  :adr/id "ADR-2608190200"
  :adr/title "A resident tick that read, attempted nothing and stopped has completed"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-19"
  :adr/body
  "## Context

ADR-2608190100 stopped one blocked resident tick from holding the single
workforce slot and freezing all 70 Bots. It said plainly that it had not
touched the reason the tick blocked, and named that as the next change. This
is it.

The reason is a conjunction of two rules that are each correct.
ADR-2608151900 admits `goal_complete` only once every plan step carries a host
execution receipt, so provider prose cannot manufacture evidence of work. The
resident objective tells a tick that finds nothing safe to do to record the
evidence for a no-op and complete. A no-op is not a tool call, so the step that
records one can never carry a receipt, and `goal_complete` is unreachable for
exactly the ticks that had nothing to do.

Measured 2026-08-19: 326 of 461 stored resident runs had failed, and all three
that ran in the first ten minutes after the fleet was unwedged blocked on this.
One of them said so itself:

  Host to accept the no-op evidence from s1 (workspace_list + git_status) as
  sufficient for goal completion, or to relax the \"fully host-verified plan\"
  requirement for no-op ticks where the second step is evidence-recording only.

## Decision

**A resident run that produced read receipts and attempted no write has
completed, and the host says so from its own records.**

`complete-resident-no-op!` already existed for a neighbouring case — a resident
tick whose provider returned nothing or died on an HTTP error, after reads. It
is generalised rather than duplicated, and now takes a reason: `:blocked` joins
`:provider/empty-response` and `:provider/http-error`. There is one no-op
decision, not two.

This is not the host agreeing with the provider's prose. Prose is still unable
to manufacture a receipt, and `goal_complete` is unchanged. What the host reads
is its own event log: reads happened, no write was attempted, therefore nothing
needed doing and nothing was done. The provider's stated prerequisite is
carried into the summary as a report, not as evidence.

**\"No write was attempted\" is now measured.** The existing path asserted it in
the sentence it wrote, and nothing checked — true for the one caller it had,
since a provider that never answered cannot have called a write, and false the
moment a second caller arrived. `attempted-nothing?` requires that no receipt
names a write tool and that no approval card is outstanding. An answerable card
is the host's own record that a write was asked for; completing over it would
answer a question that was put to a person.

**Resident only.** An interactive Goal that blocks is for the person watching
it to interpret.

**`:agent.run/result :safe-no-op`, never a plain success.** The reason travels
in the `run/no-op-completed` event. A workforce that does nothing and a
workforce that does something have to stay countable apart; recording a no-op
as ordinary success is how an idle fleet reads as a working one.

**The plan contract now says finishing is not a step.** `goal_plan` refuses by
name a step that records a conclusion, reports a no-op, or calls
`goal_complete` — no tool can produce a receipt for it, so a plan containing
one can never complete. That is prevention; the paragraphs above are the
safety net, and the net is what holds when a provider plans one anyway.

## Verification

Six tests. Each of the four changes was reverted on its own and only its own
tests went red:

  removing the wiring in `run-goal-job!`
    -> run-goal-job-routes-a-resident-block-through-the-no-op-check
  dropping the write / approval check
    -> a-resident-tick-that-wrote-is-not-called-a-no-op,
       a-resident-tick-waiting-on-an-approval-is-not-called-a-no-op
  dropping the resident guard
    -> an-interactive-block-is-never-reclassified-as-a-no-op
  reverting the plan wording
    -> the-plan-contract-says-finishing-is-not-a-step

The wiring test exists because the other five call a private function
directly, and a decision nothing calls is not reachable — the tests that reach
past the seam cannot tell the difference.

## Not settled

Whether a Bot that reports a safe no-op on every tick is working correctly or
has nothing to do is a question about the objectives in
`network-awai/loop-yakuwari`, not about this code. The `:safe-no-op` result and
its reason are what make that countable; nothing yet counts it."}]
